# plugin-local known_hosts for the ssh-gateway sidecar.
#
# Shipped EMPTY on purpose. The sidecar runs ssh with:
#   -o UserKnownHostsFile=<this file> -o StrictHostKeyChecking=yes
# so the FIRST contact with a host is NOT a silent trust-on-first-use that
# would append to your real %USERPROFILE%\.ssh\known_hosts: ssh REFUSES an
# unknown key instead of writing one, and the file it is handed is this one,
# never the user's global known_hosts.
#
# To authorise a host, seed its key here with ssh-keyscan, e.g. from a host you
# trust has already resolved the destination correctly (NOT from the sandbox):
#
#   ssh-keyscan -p 22 logs.example.invalid \
#     >> "C:\path\to\plugins\ssh-gateway\known_hosts"
#
# or pin a specific key type:
#
#   ssh-keyscan -p 22 -t ed25519 logs.example.invalid \
#     >> "C:\path\to\plugins\ssh-gateway\known_hosts"
#
# Review every line you add like code: a host key is the trust root for the
# connection, and a wrong entry means an attacker-in-the-middle ssh is not
# detected. A per-host "known_hosts" entry in the gateway JSON overrides this
# file for that one host.
